.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt SED 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm sed
.Nd stream editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Ealnru
.Ar command
.Op Fl I Ar extension
.Op Fl i Ar extension
.Op Ar
.Nm
.Op Fl Ealnru
.Op Fl e Ar command
.Op Fl f Ar command_file
.Op Fl I Ar extension
.Op Fl i Ar extension
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads each
.Ar file
.Po
.Sq Fl ,
or none at all, being the standard input
.Pc ,
applies the editing commands to each line, and writes the result to the standard
output.
The script is every
.Fl e
and
.Fl f
argument joined by newlines in the order given or, with neither, the first
operand.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Accepted for compatibility:
.Ic w
files are always created before any input is read.
.It Fl E , Fl r
Regular expressions are extended rather than basic; see
.Xr re_format 7 .
.It Fl e Ar command
Append
.Ar command
to the script.
.It Fl f Ar command_file
Append the contents of
.Ar command_file
to the script.
.It Fl I Ar extension
Edit the files in place, saving each original under its name with
.Ar extension
appended (no backup if
.Ar extension
is empty).
The files are one stream: line numbers run on, a range can span files, and
.Ql $
is the last line of the last file.
.It Fl i Ar extension
As
.Fl I ,
but each file is a stream of its own, as though edited by a separate
.Nm :
line numbers restart, ranges end, and
.Ql $
is each file's last line.
.It Fl l , Fl u
Flush the output after every line.
.It Fl n
Write the pattern space only when told to.
A script whose first line is
.Ql #n
does the same.
.El
.Pp
The extension is a separate argument:
.Ql sed -i '' file
edits without a backup.
.Ss Operation
.Nm
reads a line, without its newline, into the
.Em pattern space ,
runs each command whose addresses select it, writes the pattern space (unless
.Fl n ) ,
and starts again with the next line.
The
.Em hold space
keeps text between cycles.
A last input line without a newline is written without one.
.Ss Addresses
A command may have no address (it applies to every line), one, or two separated
by a comma.
An address is
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar number
the line with that number, counted across the input;
.It Li $
the last line;
.It Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li / , Li \e Ns Ar c Ns Ar re Ns Ar c
a line the regular expression
.Ar re
matches.
Any character but backslash and newline may stand for
.Ar c ;
.Li \e Ns Ar c
inside is a literal
.Ar c ,
and
.Li \en
a newline.
An
.Li I
after it ignores case.
The empty expression
.Li //
is the last one used.
.El
.Pp
Two addresses select a range: from a line the first selects to the next line the
second selects, or to that line itself when the second is a number not greater
than the first line's.
The second address may be
.Li + Ns Ar N ,
selecting
.Ar N
more lines.
A
.Ql \&!
after the addresses applies the command to the lines not selected.
.Ss Commands
Commands are separated by newlines or semicolons; blanks may precede them.
The commands
.Ic a , b , c , i , r , t , w , \&:
and
.Ic #
take the rest of the line.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic { Ar commands Ic }
Run
.Ar commands
on the selected lines.
.It Ic a Ns Li \e Ar text
Write
.Ar text
at the end of the cycle (or when
.Ic n
or
.Ic N
reads the next line).
.It Ic b Op Ar label
Branch to
.Ar label ,
or to the end of the script.
.It Ic c Ns Li \e Ar text
Delete the pattern space and, at the end of a range (or for each line with no
range), write
.Ar text ;
start the next cycle.
.It Ic d
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle.
.It Ic D
If the pattern space has no newline, as
.Ic d ;
otherwise delete through its first newline and start the next cycle without
reading input.
.It Ic g , Ic G
Replace the pattern space with the hold space, or append a newline and the hold
space to it.
.It Ic h , Ic H
Replace the hold space with the pattern space, or append a newline and the
pattern space to it.
.It Ic i Ns Li \e Ar text
Write
.Ar text
now.
.It Ic l
Write the pattern space unambiguously: backslash escapes for
.Ql \e\e ,
alert, backspace, form feed, newline, carriage return, tab and vertical tab,
three-digit octal for other non-printing bytes, long lines folded with
.Ql \e ,
and
.Ql $
at the end.
.It Ic n
Write the pattern space (unless
.Fl n )
and replace it with the next line; with none left, quit.
.It Ic N
Append a newline and the next line to the pattern space; with none left, quit
without writing it.
.It Ic p
Write the pattern space.
.It Ic P
Write the pattern space up to its first newline.
.It Ic q
Write the pattern space (unless
.Fl n )
and any appended text, and quit.
.It Ic r Ar file
Write the contents of
.Ar file
at the end of the cycle; a file that can't be read is nothing.
.It Ic s Ns Li / Ns Ar re Ns Li / Ns Ar replacement Ns Li / Ns Ar flags
Replace the match of
.Ar re
with
.Ar replacement ,
in which
.Ql &
is the match,
.Li \e1
to
.Li \e9
its subexpressions, and
.Li \en
or a backslash before a newline a newline.
Any character but backslash and newline may delimit.
The flags are
.Cm g
(every match),
.Ar N
(the
.Ar N Ns th
match; with
.Cm g ,
it and every later one),
.Cm p
(write the pattern space if replaced),
.Cm i
or
.Cm I
(ignore case), and
.Cm w Ar file
(append the pattern space to
.Ar file
if replaced).
.It Ic t Op Ar label
Branch to
.Ar label
(or the end) if a substitution was made since the last line was read or
.Ic t
taken.
.It Ic w Ar file
Append the pattern space to
.Ar file .
Each
.Ar file
is created, empty, before input is read;
.Pa /dev/stdout
and
.Pa /dev/stderr
are the standard output and error.
.It Ic x
Exchange the pattern and hold spaces.
.It Ic y Ns Li / Ns Ar string1 Ns Li / Ns Ar string2 Ns Li /
Replace each character of
.Ar string1
with the one at the same place in
.Ar string2 .
.It Ic \&: Ns Ar label
A label for
.Ic b
and
.Ic t .
.It Ic =
Write the line number.
.It Ic #
A comment, to the end of the line.
.El
.Pp
The text of
.Ic a ,
.Ic c
and
.Ic i
follows a backslash and newline, or, on the same line, the command; each line of
it but the last ends with a backslash.
Leading blanks of each text line are dropped, and a backslash before any other
character keeps that character.
.Pp
Labels and file names run to the end of the line, semicolons included.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Replace every
.Ql foo
with
.Ql bar
in a file, keeping a backup:
.Dl $ sed -i .orig 's/foo/bar/g' file
.Pp
Print lines 10 to 20:
.Dl $ sed -n '10,20p' file
.Pp
Join each line ending in a backslash with the next:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sed -e ':a' -e '/\e\e$/N; s/\e\e\en//; ta' file
.Ed
.Pp
Collapse runs of repeated lines, as
.Xr uniq 1 :
.Dl $ sed '$!N; /^\e(.*\e)\en\e1$/!P; D'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr awk 1 ,
.Xr ed 1 ,
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr re_format 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq), whose options are
.Fl E ,
.Fl e ,
.Fl f
and
.Fl n .
.Fl a , I , i , l , r
and
.Fl u ,
the
.Li I
address and
.Cm i Ns / Ns Cm I
substitution flags, and
.Li + Ns Ar N
addresses are those of
.Fx .
.Pp
As POSIX requires, a delimiter escaped inside a regular expression is that
character literally (so in
.Ql s.a\e.b.X.\&
the dot matches only a dot), and
.Ic N
on the last line quits without writing the pattern space.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v7 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
